\title{The Sequencher project format}
\description{
Sequencher project files (\code{.SPF}) are undocumented. This page describes the
parts of the format that \code{\link[=read_spf]{read_spf()}} relies on, as worked out from a
project saved by Sequencher 5.4 on a Mac. For the 150 reads in that project
whose original \code{.ab1} files survive, \code{\link[=read_spf]{read_spf()}} reproduces the processed
traces, peak positions and quality scores exactly. The 14 projects published
with two studies (doi:10.1371/journal.pgen.1006176 and
\href{https://zenodo.org/records/5557681}{Zenodo record 5557681}) have no original
files to compare against, but all 2,214 of their reads have one peak
position per base, all within the trace, and the called base is the
strongest trace channel at its peak for 94.8\% of the 2.0 million A, C, G and
T calls. Projects from other versions may differ.
}
\details{
All integers are big-endian and all offsets are absolute byte positions in
the file, counted from 0.
}
\section{Overall layout}{
The file is a heap of tagged records and data blocks, with no table of
contents at a fixed position. Records point at their data blocks by file
offset, so the blocks for one read are scattered through the file and do not
sit next to its record. Text such as read names, file paths, instrument names
and annotation labels is stored null-terminated. In projects saved on a Mac,
characters outside ASCII are Mac Roman: \code{c6} is ∆, as in \verb{est2∆}. The
encoding of projects saved on Windows is not known; \code{\link[=read_spf]{read_spf()}} and
\code{\link[=read_spf_contigs]{read_spf_contigs()}} decode all text as Mac Roman.

Records and blocks start with a 6-byte tag: a class byte, a version byte of
\code{30}, \code{31} or \code{32}, and \verb{00 00 00 01}. In object tables and member lists the
class byte has bit \code{02} set when the version byte is \code{30} (\code{92}, \code{d2}) and
clear otherwise (\code{90}, \code{d0}). Both forms can occur in one file.
}

\section{Read records}{
Each imported trace has one record that starts with the 10 bytes
\verb{c0 31 00 00 00 01 63 43 72 6d} (the last four spell \code{cCrm}), or with \code{32}
in place of \code{31}. The layout that follows is the same in both. Records
that start with \code{c2} instead appear to be reads deleted from the project:
their block tables point at space that has since been reused, and they are
skipped. Deleted reads can also keep the \code{c0} tag; see "Deleted records"
below.
\code{\link[=read_spf]{read_spf()}} finds reads by searching for these tags. Relative to the start of
the tag:\tabular{lll}{
   Offset \tab Size \tab Contents \cr
   44 \tab 4 \tab offset of the read's name, the file name it was imported under \cr
   48 \tab 4 \tab length of the name, including the terminating null \cr
   259 \tab 64 \tab block table: 8 pairs of (offset, length), 4 bytes each \cr
}


The block table lists, in order:\tabular{lll}{
   Slot \tab Contents \tab Length field \cr
   1 \tab base calls \tab number of bases \cr
   2 \tab quality scores; offset and length are 0 if the read has none \tab number of bases \cr
   3 \tab unused; offset and length are 0 \tab  \cr
   4 \tab peak positions, a \code{DnFx} block \tab compressed size \cr
   5–8 \tab A, C, G and T trace channels, \code{DnFx} blocks \tab compressed size \cr
}


The name shown in Sequencher can differ from the original file name: reads
can be renamed after import, and the record keeps only the current name.
}

\section{Base calls and quality scores}{
Base calls take one byte each, holding a 4-bit mask with A = 1, C = 2, G = 4
and T = 8. Ambiguity codes are the sums of these (for example R = A + G = 5),
and N is 15. \code{\link[=read_spf]{read_spf()}} maps the masks to IUPAC letters and maps 0 to \code{-}.
In the published projects, the two bases of a two-base code are the two
strongest trace channels at that peak for 105 of 117 calls. These are
the calls as edited in Sequencher, so they can differ from the original file
where bases were inserted, deleted or changed.

Quality scores take one byte each and are the original Phred scores.
}

\section{\code{DnFx} blocks}{
Peak positions and trace channels are stored as compressed series of 16-bit
integers. Each block starts with a 24-byte header:\tabular{lll}{
   Offset \tab Size \tab Contents \cr
   0 \tab 4 \tab the ASCII tag \code{DnFx} \cr
   4 \tab 4 \tab uncompressed size in bytes, two per value \cr
   8 \tab 4 \tab size of the payload that follows the header \cr
   12 \tab 4 \tab unknown; varies between blocks, possibly a checksum \cr
   16 \tab 4 \tab \verb{48 b7 ff bf} for peak positions, \verb{68 b7 ff bf} for traces \cr
   20 \tab 1 \tab 0 \cr
   21 \tab 1 \tab order k of the difference predictor, 2 for peaks and 3–5 for traces \cr
   22 \tab 2 \tab 0 \cr
}


The payload starts with a chunk index:
\itemize{
\item bytes 0–1: the number of complete 256-value chunks, \code{floor(n / 256)};
\item bytes 2–3: \verb{01 00} in every block seen;
\item one 14-byte entry per chunk, holding the last five values of the chunk as
16-bit integers, a flag byte (\code{0x80} or \code{0x00}, meaning unknown) and a
3-byte position in the residual stream counted in nibbles.
}

The index allows random access into a long trace. \code{\link[=read_spf]{read_spf()}} does not
need it, because the residual stream that follows the index decodes from
start to finish without breaks at chunk boundaries.
}

\section{Predictor}{
Each value is predicted from the k values before it by a k-th order
difference: for k = 2 the prediction is \code{2 * x[i - 1] - x[i - 2]}, and in
general it is the sum over j = 1, ..., k of
\code{(-1)^(j + 1) * choose(k, j) * x[i - j]}. Values before the start of the
series count as 0. The stream stores the residual, the value minus its
prediction, so the series is recovered by summing the residuals cumulatively
k times.
}

\section{Residual coding}{
The residual stream is read four bits at a time, high nibble first.
\itemize{
\item A nibble from \code{0} to \code{7} or from \code{9} to \code{f} is a residual from -7 to 7,
read as a signed 4-bit integer.
\item The nibble \code{8} switches to bytes for one or more residuals. In each byte
the top bit is set on the last residual of the run, after which the stream
returns to nibbles. The low seven bits are a signed integer s, which stands
for a residual of s + 7 if s > 0 and s - 7 if s < 0.
\item If s = 0, the next four nibbles hold a signed 16-bit integer S, which
stands for S + 7 or S - 7 in the same way. Large residuals at the start of
a series are stored this way.
}

For example, \verb{3 d 0 7} is the residuals 3, -3, 0 and 7; \verb{8 81} is 8 and
\verb{8 ff} is -8; \verb{8 7e 06 fb} is the run -9, 13, -12.
}

\section{Object IDs}{
Records refer to one another by 32-bit object ID rather than by offset.
Object tables are tagged \code{92} or \code{90}, then hold a 16-bit count and that
many pairs of (file offset, ID). Pages of member IDs (below) share the \code{90}
tag; \code{\link[=read_spf_contigs]{read_spf_contigs()}} takes a block as an object table only if every
offset in it starts a record. Some IDs that records refer to are missing
from the tables as counted, but their (offset, ID) pairs still occur in the
file; \code{\link[=read_spf_contigs]{read_spf_contigs()}} finds them by searching for the ID directly after
an offset that starts a record.
}

\section{Contigs}{
Contig records start with \verb{c0 31 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00}, or with \code{32} in
place of \code{31}. Relative to the start of the record:\tabular{lll}{
   Offset \tab Size \tab Contents \cr
   28 \tab 1 \tab record type: 2 for a contig, 4 for a placement record \cr
   48 \tab 1 \tab number of members \cr
   49 \tab 4 \tab offset of the member list \cr
   53 \tab 4 \tab unknown; 11 in every contig seen \cr
   71 \tab 8 \tab offset and length of the consensus block \cr
   205 \tab 8 \tab offset and length of the contig's name, null-terminated \cr
}


A member list tagged \code{d2} or \code{d0} holds a 16-bit count and one 4-byte object
ID per member. A list tagged \code{52} or \code{50} is paged: its count is the number
of pages, followed by the file offset of each page, and each page is a block
tagged \code{92} or \code{90} with a count and up to 32 member IDs. Records of type 0
use the same layout; in some projects one of them, named after the project
file, lists the contigs.

Each member is a placement record, which holds
the object ID of its read or reference sequence at offset 79. Reference
sequences (records tagged \code{cBAS}, two bases per byte) have no name of their
own; the placement record gives it at offset 205.

The consensus block holds one byte per alignment column. The low four bits
are the base mask used for reads, and 0 where the column has no consensus
base; the meaning of the high four bits is unknown. It is the consensus as
last saved, not one recomputed from the reads. In the Sequencher 5.4 project,
every contig held only reads of the accession it was named after, plus the
reference. In the 14 published projects, every read that is not deleted
belongs to a contig, and each contig's member count at offset 48 matches its
member list. A contig record that starts with \code{c2}, like the deleted reads,
had no live reads and is skipped.
}

\section{Deleted records}{
Sequencher can leave the records of deleted reads and contigs in the file,
with their \code{c0} tags and data intact. \code{\link[=read_spf]{read_spf()}} and \code{\link[=read_spf_contigs]{read_spf_contigs()}}
count a record as deleted when no object table lists it, and return such
records only with \code{deleted = TRUE}. In 14 of the 16 projects examined, an
object table lists every read. One published project lists all but one, and
the unlisted read has the same traces as a listed one, so it is an old copy.
The last project lists 4 of its 30 reads. The other 26 decode cleanly; 24
carry names from a 2011 sequencing plate, and two have names overwritten by
later data. \code{\link[=read_spf]{read_spf()}} names such reads \verb{unnamed_read_<n>}.
}

\section{Not yet decoded}{
The position, orientation and gapped sequence of each read within its
contig, which the placement records also hold; annotation records (tagged
\verb{c2 31 00 00}) such as the end trimming applied by Sequencher; and import
records tagged \verb{<ID:}.
}

